{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1513762-sorcerous-societies-new-revised-sects-orders","id":1513762,"title":"Sorcerous Societies [New/Revised Sects/Orders]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-06-03T06:01:41-07:00","id":1513762,"title":"Sorcerous Societies [New/Revised Sects/Orders]","content":"This is an idea that has been buzzing in my head for quite some time. In general, I've always liked to ponder about how to hack settings and rebuild them, and one of the things that I always liked about the CofD is the occult organizations that it tends to feature. This is probably the reason why Hunter is my favorite gameline, as not only that it feature such organizations, it also actively encourage you to make your own homebrew. Mage, on the other hand, was a significantly more structured (especially in its early stages) due to the metaphysical Truths which the orders are meant to represent. Later Mage products, and especially 2e, have made shaken things up, but the game still remains on the cohesive society side of CofD games.<br /> <br /> Now, don't get me wrong- I don't think that having a cohesive, structured society is a bad thing. Between Legacies, Nameless Orders and Factions, players and storytellers have a wide set of tools if they wish to expand their setting, while the setting gains a lot from having a solid, organized structure that maintains the themes that the game wishes to tell. However, the itch in my brain keeps on itching, especially after Tome of the Pentacle have dropped all while introducing a in depth history of the Awakened, the local views of mages around the world, and the Lost Orders. Now, while scratching the itch could be a good enough reason to start pondering around with the setting, without a defined purpose it would be (a) harder to focus on what exactly this project is meant to be (b) help others understand the goal of this project and whether they are interested in it beyond a general &quot;homebrew setting lol&quot;. As such, I think that I should start with some well defined goals of what this project is meant to achieve, so both you (and I) will know better how to finally get rid of that annoying itch.<br /> <br /> <b>1) As Above, So Below:</b> Imperial Mysteries is, like, a <i>really</i> cool book imo, adding a whole new level to the game by going full blown Cosmic, and one of the interesting features it adds is the introduction of the Ententes- those divine alliances between godlike entities who struggle against one another over holding the fabric of reality. This concept is nice considering how it parallels the Sects which compose the Awakened society.. to a degree. I mean, we have the Exemplars, who represent and support the values of the Pentacle Alliance, the Tetrarchs, who stand at the head of the Iron Pyramid, and the Aswadim, who explore the depths of the Abyss for possibilities found outside of the Truth and are viewed as gods by the Scelesti. However, that's only half of the Ententes, with the others being not as easily mapped into the Fallen World. The Alienated, who worship the Old Gods of the Supernal, sound like something that could be easily developed and distinguished into its own pseudo-Sect, in a similar manner to the Obelisk- but then we have the Siddhas, who seek to embody the Arcana themselves, or the Bodhisattvas, who serve the Oracles and seek to progress humanity towards Ascension which both overlaps and is very different from the goal of the Orders? Those groups are not easily mapped into a distinguished Sects, and tying to Supernal Truths to the Fallen Lie sounds like something would be very satisfying to me.<br /> <br /> <b>2) Lost in Translation:</b> Awakening has a very distinct identity which differs a lot from its predecessor, Ascension, to the point that one could probably make a setting with the two different types of Mages coexisting in the same world. However, while the two are indeed different, we can clearly see Ascension's fingertips over Awakening, such as the Tremere, the Ascension War, the Nefandi, Aswadim and even the Technocratic Union. Now, a lot of those resemblances are merely shout outs, using same names in a very different context- but that only makes me think how we could harvest other features from Ascension in a manner that would still feel unique and fitting to the Awakening setting. The Translation Guide offers some help with such adaption (honestly, in an ideal world I would have loved to get more of that series, but oh well), which further makes me interested in taking the elements from Ascension and port them into Awakening in an (hopefully) interesting way, including some of the Traditions and Factions of the game as Sects and Orders (not necessary correspondingly).<br /> <br /> <b>3) What if:</b> Isn't this an interesting question to ask- what if? What if the Awakened history did not take the same turns and twists in did in the canon? What if the Great Cults were to maintain their own distinct identity over the philosophies of the Awakened? What if the Mysterium were not as unified as it is in modern time, still allowing both the Keepers and the Pancryptiates their own distinguished identity? What if the Great Refusal were to not be as effective as it was in the canon? What if the Vedmak were to be accepted as an Order and were to avoid the Tremere trap? What if Nassau were to survive the joined attacks of the Seers and the Ladder? In a setting of false histories, erased pasts and rewritten reality, the multiverse and the questions it rise are something hidden right beneath the surface, and the Tome of the Pentacle emphasis it in a very clear manner, speaking about events which never were in order to expose the Lie which the Sleepers call as &quot;history&quot;.<br /> <br /> <b>4) Blurry Lines:</b> there is something a bit, let's say, &quot;funky&quot; in how Sects are presented in the setting. I mean, in early 1e, we had a very clear division- mages were either a Pentacle or a Seer. Easy and simple. However, as the setting evolved and developed, things became much less easy to define- the Pentacle was a modern creation from the get go, and the separation between the Diamond and the Free Council became more and more apparent as time progressed, to the point that in the current state, it is more true to say that we have 3 Sects- the Diamond, the Council and the Seers, with the integration of the Pentacle being a &quot;work in progress&quot; as we can see with the crumbling of the Diamond Astral Realm (which may be replaced with something else... or not. Who knows?). This is better reinforced by the Free Council having its Assemblies and the Seers having their Ministries, which help both feel large and present enough in contrast to the Diamond and its Orders... however, both the Council and the Seers are also Orders, having a singular Order Merit, even though those ones are easily adaptable to many personal communities. Still, it creates a situation where it starts making you ask &quot;when does a Order becomes a Sect&quot;? After all, the Diamond Orders have their own Factions, very different ideologies and do not always work together in harmony. What does it take for a Order to become a Sect is a question that has been bugging me for a while, and that I plan on trying to answer for myself in here.<br /> <br /> <b>5) Remnants:</b> not everything that was presented in 1e has been equally adapted to 2e. The most glaring example for this is the way Banishers are presented between the editions, moving from a secret pseudo-Order into a phenomenon + a few random Legacies. Note, I don't think that the transition was <i>bad</i> for the setting as a whole. The material given about Banishers in the new edition, such as Harrowing Awakening, is really good, and as the setting started to move from Atlantis as a actual entity lost to time and more towards treating it as a symbol, the &quot;role&quot; of the Banishers in the setting have changed. So I do agree that looking on the final product, the change was probably for the better- but I can't deny that I really liked the Banishers lore in 1e, including the Timori, Locust Eaters, their treatment as a pseudo-Order and so forth. As such, porting them, together with potentially other concepts that were left scattered around in 1e (such as Magical Traditions or the Blood of the Dragon) and have not been fully realized in 2e, sounds like a subject of interest in this project.<br /> <br /> <b>6) Unified Occult Society Theory:</b> so as I said in the beginning, I like the occult societies that CofD has to offer, and for a long time I have hacked and revised all kinds of ideas about how to adapt them all under a singular paradigm. Organization rules, obviously, are helpful mechanically, but I am less interested in mechanical unification and more in a thematic unification. Up to this point, I have mostly used Hunter's perception to do so, restructuring the Tiers and redefining types of organizations in order to fold groups from other gamelines into a single unified setting- something which felt natural for Hunter considering, again, its flexibility in the subject. However, through the use of Sects and such, it makes sense that once I start doing some rewrites to how Orders work in Mage, I would feel the same itch to try and use this setting to fold other occult societies under the same umbrella, especially ones that have some very strong interaction with the gameline- such as the Sons of Phobos from Vampire or the Shield and Spear from Hunter. That also includes trying to adapt the special sorcerous methodologies of other gamelines, such as Blood Sorcery, Sekhem Sorcery or Alchemy, into a singular system to present the methods of &quot;foreign sorcery&quot; (much to the Tremere's delight, obviously).<br /> <br /> <b>7) Welcome to Atlantis:</b> both a mirror project and a motivation to this one, one of the projects I have in the pile of &quot;things I need to do&quot; is the Atlantis Shard that I have been working on for some time. Basically, it is a reimagining of the setting from the Atlantean point of view, presenting a vision of Atlantis as a utopia of magic and wonder on the verge of failure, all while reflecting the Fallen reality that the Awakened live in. As such, it requires some good correspondence of the different factions into Atlantean symbolism- including enemy factions and hostile entities. While it can relay solely on the current setting, as it draws from all kinds of places in the setting, including Imperial Mysteries, it also creates a feedback loop that further demands changes in the core setting to completely fit the vision I have for that shard. Basically, we have a feedback loop in here. As such, every entry in here will not cover how the Order and Sects work within the &quot;standard&quot; setting but also how they work within Atlantis. Expect the use of many dragon parts in this one.<br /> <br /> So yeah, this is basically a 7-part explanation for the motivation (very Tremere appropriate). While I thought to actually start tackling the project in this post, I think that it is enough for people who may be interested to get a grasp of what it is meant to be and comment appropriately. Instead, I will post a outline of my current plans for the project, just so you will know to where we plan on going with this and comment accordingly if you have ideas or suggestions:<br /> <br /> <u><b>Outline</b></u><br /> <br /> <b><a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1a5eO3BAN5J_wA9qPy__kTlL9LU5OKptZIx_Q2XZXtOY/edit#\" target=\"_blank\">Broken Diamond</a>: </b>alternative Diamond Orders and the Mysterium and Guardians as Sects.<br /> <br /> <b>Humanity's Best and Worst:</b> Free Council restructured<br /> <br /> <b>Top of the Pyramid:</b> Seers analysis<br /> <br /> <b>Shattered Obelisk:</b> Scelesti analysis<br /> <br /> <b>Gnawing at the World: </b>Tremere analysis<br /> \u200b<br /> <b>War in Heaven:</b> Verbena, Celestial Chorus and the Traditions<br /> <br /> <b>Shadow of the Council:</b> Technocratic Union<br /> <br /> <b>Those Left Behind:</b> Nameless Orders<br /> <br /> <b>Death of Magic:</b> Banishers and Hunters<br /> <br /> <b>Ashes of Ascension:</b> Bacchanal (Marauders) and Nephandi<br /> <br /> <b>Thieves and Tricksters:</b> Promethean Brotherhood, Cheiron, Faithful of Shulpae and Hototogisu Oh My<br /> <br /> <b>Watchers From the Outside:</b> non Supernal magic and dedicated occult organizations<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Well, that's it for now. I hope you will find this of interest. I can't commit that things will be posted according to the order of the timeline and that things won;'t change as the project progress, but feel free to already give suggestions and your own ideas about the project. Hope you'll find it of interest!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","user":"LostLight","date":". 06-05-2023, 06:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6},{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-nam